This project investigates and compares three distinct families of generative models—Variational Autoencoders (VAE), Deep Convolutional Generative Adversarial Networks (DCGAN), and Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPM)—for synthesizing realistic artwork. Trained and evaluated on the ArtBench-10 dataset of 32x32 paintings, the models were initially developed on a 20% subset before the best-performing architecture was scaled to the full dataset. The evaluation protocol employed Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) and Kernel Inception Distance (KID) to assess image fidelity and diversity. The results highlight the trade-offs of each approach, showing that while VAEs offer stable training, DCGANs provide superior visual sharpness and distribution coverage, achieving the best FID score in the study's constrained computational environment.
